# Hotel project standards

## Prisma

- Never run `prisma migrate` or `prisma db push`.
- `prisma generate` is allowed when the schema changes.

## Quality checks

- Do not run `npm run build` (or equivalent full builds) only to check lint or types. Use the editor/linter or targeted checks.

## Code style

- Write clean, senior-level code: correct formatting, minimal scope, no dead code.
- Add files and helpers only when they earn their place; do not split or abstract prematurely.

## Folder layout

- `app/` — routes only: pages, layouts, and API route handlers.
- `components/` (repo root) — all reusable UI. Do not add `_components` or other component folders under `app/`.
- `lib/`, `types/`, etc. — shared non-UI logic.

## Auth — API routes

- Non-public API handlers must use `withAdminAuth` from `@/lib/auth`.
- Public APIs (e.g. NextAuth at `/api/auth/*`) skip admin checks.
- Page protection is separate (see below); do not rely on API guards for page access alone.

## Auth — pages

- Protected admin pages must use `withAdminSession` from `@/lib/auth` at the page level (HOC), not ad-hoc `auth()` + redirect in every file.
- Login and other intentionally public admin routes stay unwrapped.

## Middleware

- Use `proxy.ts` (not `middleware.ts`) for edge middleware: redirects, matchers, and request-level auth gating. Keep business logic in `lib/` and route handlers.

## Deprecations and breaking changes

- Never add `@deprecated`, silent aliases, or remove/rename exports without asking the user first.
- If the user approves deprecation, ask: replace all usages, or leave old code commented for reference?
- If you made a questionable choice (extra abstraction, alias, dead code, scope creep), say so briefly in the reply — do not hide it.

## Replies to the user

- Default to short answers: what changed and why, in a few lines.
- Do not paste large code blocks or repeat existing code unless the user asked for it.
The below rule is super strict and you will follow it in 99.99% time unless asked to do something in detail and talk in detail
- Skip long summaries, checklists of every file touched, and restating rules back to the user.
